Charge the pipeline refill of IRQ entry, unless a DMA hides it.

runtime_tick takes a pending, unmasked IRQ at the first instruction
boundary and calls runtime_irq, which sets the PC to the vector 0x18
without charging any cycle; only a wake from HALT adds
kIrqWakeDelayCycles. Entering an IRQ writes the PC, and mGBA charges that
write like any other: ARMRaiseIRQ does cpu->cycles += ARMWritePC(cpu),
2 cycles plus the N and S fetch waits at the vector, 2 in the zero-wait
BIOS (mGBA 0.10.5 src/arm/arm.c:123-139, include/mgba/internal/arm/
isa-inlines.h:73-81). Except while a DMA blocks the CPU: mGBA's event loop
then advances the CPU straight to its next event (GBAProcessEvents,
src/gba/gba.c:300-339, `mTimingTick(..., cycles < nextEvent ? nextEvent :
cycles)` while cpuBlocked), and the 2 cycles pass within that wait.

So in code that takes many IRQs, gbarecomp ran 2 cycles ahead of mGBA per
IRQ taken while no DMA ran, and that adds up within a frame: in a game
whose HBlank IRQ fires on every line, about 136 cycles per frame over the
68 lines of VBlank (on the visible lines the HBlank DMA hides them in
both). Enough to move a VCOUNT read across a line boundary now and then.

The change, in runtime_tick (runtime_bus_bridge.cpp): before runtime_irq,
charge 2 + runtime_refill_cycles(0x18, ARM), the same refill a generated
PC write charges, unless drain_dma_steal charged DMA cycles in the same
runtime_tick (it now returns how many). The cycles are pumped as the HALT
wake delay is: devices advance, IRQs raised meanwhile stay pending in IF,
and the IRQ already decided is taken. A wake from HALT now costs the 7
cycles of kIrqWakeDelayCycles plus the 2 of the entry, as in mGBA (the IRQ
event's delay, then ARMRaiseIRQ). runtime_refill_cycles also empties the
GamePak prefetch buffer, as for any PC write, when the prefetch reset
patch (0011) is applied; on its own this patch only adds the cycles.

"A DMA in the same runtime_tick" approximates mGBA's "a DMA blocks the
CPU when the IRQ is raised": gbarecomp charges a DMA's stolen cycles at
once, where mGBA starts it 3 cycles after its trigger, and takes an IRQ at
the next instruction boundary, where mGBA takes it 7 cycles after it is
raised (13 for HBlank). Those latencies are not copied here. Multiplayer
session execution takes IRQs in runtime_should_yield, not here, and is
unchanged; so is tools/bios_smoke, which keeps its own IRQ loop.

Tested on macOS arm64 (Apple clang 21) at gbarecomp 5e89943 plus this
series:

- gbarecomp's ctest, 52 of 52 (with the gba-tests test ROMs).
- A synthetic 4 KiB ROM (no Nintendo data, header logo zero), irqentry1,
  times 100 iterations of `subs; bne` from the cartridge between two timer
  0 reads at F/1, alone and with one timer 1 IRQ inside (no DMA, the
  handler in the cartridge). mGBA 0.10.5 on a minimal stand-in BIOS
  (vectors, an IRQ dispatcher, VBlankIntrWait): 2596 and 2748 cycles.
  gbarecomp 5e89943 on the same image: 2596 and 2746, on generated code
  (GBARECOMP_STRICT_STATIC=1, fully static) and on the interpreter
  (GBARECOMP_FORCE_INTERP=1, BIOS interpreted too). With this patch: 2596
  and 2748 on both. On the retail BIOS, mGBA gives 2596 and 2747. The
  generators and config are in tests/repro/ of
  https://github.com/nvergez/moto-racer-advanced-browser (make_rom.py,
  make_bios.py, irqentry1.toml); attach them when filing this patch.
- A commercial game (HBlank IRQ on every line, HBlank DMA on the visible
  lines), on the interpreter, with the prefetch reset (0011 and its
  arm-recomp-core half): over one race frame, the IRQ entries on VBlank
  lines cost 2 cycles in mGBA (58 of 60 measured) and 0 without this
  patch, 2 (60 of 61) with it; on visible lines 30 in both, with and
  without. Without it the game's random number generator, which adds
  VCOUNT to its state, read one line earlier than in mGBA at the first
  draw near a line boundary, and the race diverged; with it, the
  generator's state equals mGBA's on every frame of a full race, and a
  three-race cup ends with mGBA's positions and points.

